      en: "Jahra Caravan Station served as a vital rest stop along the historic trade route connecting Basra to Najd. Its construction in the 1700s facilitated the movement of goods such as frankincense, myrrh, and Arabian horses. The station's design included a large central courtyard surrounded by rooms for traders and storage. Water was sourced from nearby wells, crucial for survival in the arid climate. The site declined with the advent of motorized transport in the early 20th century. Today, it stands as a testament to Kuwait's role in regional commerce.",
